<div><h3>Do I need a separate SMS tool for customer stories?</h3><p>Customer Stories rarely needs specialist SMS day one; add it when texts measurably beat email on customer stories. Vet customer stories consent capture, quiet-hour enforcement, and per-text cost first.</p></div>
<div><h3>How should I suppress audiences in customer stories flows?</h3><p>Guard customer stories with exits for buyers, open cases, refunds, and no-consent profiles, auditable per customer stories flow. In customer stories, a suppression miss hurts worse than a missed blast.</p></div>
<div><h3>What does customer stories email cost?</h3><p>Costs combine the platform subscription, contact or send overages, SMS credits, template and creative work, and the discount budget your customer stories campaigns consume. Plans and limits shift often, so check the official pricing pages for your customer stories shortlist and model an eighteen-month total before committing.</p></div>
<div><h3>Which app should a lean team pilot first for customer stories?</h3><p>Open customer stories with the tool at full command in fourteen days — simple customer stories sends natively, branched customer stories on a lean platform. Shipping customer stories beats architecting it.</p></div>
<div><h3>How do I measure customer stories email results?</h3><p>For customer stories, judge the program on margin per send, repeat purchase, unsubscribe and complaint rates, and support load next to attributed revenue — and keep a holdout group beside recipients so seasonal lift is never misread as program impact.</p></div>
<div><h3>Can I run customer stories email without an agency?</h3><p>Yes, if the scope stays small. Pick one customer stories journey, document consent and suppression rules, and reuse a simple template system. For customer stories, hire help only if journey complexity, inbox-placement remediation, or peak customer stories volume genuinely exceeds what the team can run.</p></div>
<div><h3>When should I graduate from my first app for customer stories?</h3><p>The customer stories moment to graduate arrives when safe editing, purchase-state segmentation, or cost forecasting at your customer stories contact scale stops working. For customer stories, that moment usually arrives when more than two people maintain flows or when peak campaigns require documented suppression.</p></div>
<div><h3>How much discounting is acceptable for customer stories?</h3><p>Treat discounts as one lever, not the default. For customer stories, test content-led recovery and loyalty first, cap discount depth against margin, and document who can approve exceptions. If most customer stories revenue needs a code, the customer stories program has a value problem rather than a pricing problem.</p></div>
<div><h3>Which Shopify data matters most for customer stories?</h3><p>Order and refund state, cart and browse events, consent source, and product availability cover most customer stories decisions. On customer stories, prove events in a test checkout before trusting them in flows, with customer stories field definitions written where both teams can find them.</p></div>
<div><h3>How do I avoid duplicate sends across apps for customer stories?</h3><p>Give one platform ownership of each customer stories journey, document which app sends what, and share suppression lists where the tools support it. For customer stories peak weeks, sample customer timelines weekly and enumerate all customer stories sends per profile.</p></div>
<div><h3>What should a customer stories pilot include?</h3><p>Bound the customer stories pilot to one audience and one or two journeys, with written suppression rules, a holdout group, and a thirty-day margin-and-complaints review — and lock customer stories success criteria before launch so nobody reinterprets results afterward.</p></div>
